1
00:00:01,582 --> 00:00:16,835
This video explains the north-star architecture of TransactHub: how the designer studio and the runtime fit together, and why they are built this way. There are no screens in this video, only pictures. Where something is not built yet, we will say so.

2
00:00:18,449 --> 00:00:32,059
The whole design rests on one idea. A payment is data, not code. A scheme or a bank is never written into shared code. A scheme is a leg, and a bank is configuration: values, hooks and port bindings.

3
00:00:33,668 --> 00:00:46,702
At the centre is one model: ISO 20 022, the international standard for payment messages. Every workflow works on one canonical context built on it. The database is shaped on it too.

4
00:00:48,316 --> 00:01:03,612
A scheme speaks its own format. The wages protection system, WPS, sends salary files and acknowledgements in its own layout. Only map blocks touch another format: one map reads the file in, another writes the reply out.

5
00:01:05,224 --> 00:01:22,781
A field the standard has no place for is never left untyped. It becomes a registered extension, typed supplementary data. And the context carries ids, not people: names and accounts are read from the store by id, so the engine's history holds no personal data.

6
00:01:24,384 --> 00:01:41,173
Next, the store. There is one store, with one model, on SQL Server or on PostgreSQL. The choice is made in one place. A build test fails if a database driver or raw SQL appears anywhere else, and every store test runs on both databases.

7
00:01:42,789 --> 00:01:58,639
The store is insert-only. A payment's status is never overwritten. Each change is a new fact, and the current status is worked out from the facts. Each fact records the hash of the fact before it, so a check can name the first fact that was changed.

8
00:02:00,241 --> 00:02:15,622
In a deployment the database itself enforces this. The hosts sign in as a login that may read and insert, and is refused an update or a delete. On the laptop stack a test proves it, after a whole switch-over between two distributions.

9
00:02:17,239 --> 00:02:26,924
There is no table per scheme and no table per bank. A scheme and a bank are columns. That is what lets a new scheme arrive without a change to the database.

10
00:02:28,528 --> 00:02:44,464
Now the engines: the part that runs a workflow for days, waits for answers, and survives restarts. TransactHub has four engines behind one contract. The studio exports one expanded workflow, and one interpreter runs it on each engine.

11
00:02:46,072 --> 00:03:03,970
One conformance kit, fifty-seven scenarios, proves they behave the same. On the twenty-third of November, DTFx and Elsa passed all fifty-seven. Each installation runs one engine. Temporal is the default, running on SQL Server through a plugin the team keeps.

12
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:23,158
One gap to know about. On DTFx and Elsa, the long-running host runs each payment in a run of its own, and that run does not yet survive a restart of its host. Flowable deploys per payment. Temporal has neither limit. This is Issue I 165.

13
00:03:24,761 --> 00:03:31,161
How does new work reach a running bank? There are three layers, each changed by different people, in a different way.

14
00:03:32,767 --> 00:03:50,068
The second layer is the distribution: a complete, signed copy of everything one bank runs. The platform release, the scheme releases the bank licenses, the bank's own pack, and the engine binding, resolved together by the studio. It is the only way new workflow code arrives.

15
00:03:51,684 --> 00:04:05,892
The third layer is the configuration release: the bank's values. Dials within their bounds, rule values, port bindings and calendars, each dated from when it applies. It changes values only, never which code runs.

16
00:04:07,503 --> 00:04:25,764
Why three? If a scheme were deployed on its own, the runtime would combine versions at run time, and every pair would need testing. If everything were one package, a fix to one scheme would redeploy every bank. And if values were inside the distribution, a limit change would need a deployment.

17
00:04:27,368 --> 00:04:43,005
So the authoring units in the studio stay small: the platform pack, each scheme pack, each bank pack, each with its own version. The deployment unit is the distribution, and the studio refuses to build one whose scheme does not fit its platform's slot.

18
00:04:44,614 --> 00:05:00,336
A payment is pinned to a distribution at acceptance. The runtime records the active distribution's id on the payment, and that never changes. Every leg, activity and rule of that payment runs from it. A payment never mixes distributions.

19
00:05:01,944 --> 00:05:15,512
Now a second distribution, B, is installed beside it. Each has its own copy: its own workflow definitions and, on Temporal, its own worker build id. Activating B moves A to draining, in the same step.

20
00:05:17,123 --> 00:05:28,259
From that instant, new payments take B. Payment 1 keeps running on A, to its end, even if an answer from the scheme arrives days later: the answer is handed to the host of A.

21
00:05:29,865 --> 00:05:46,121
When no payment pinned to A is open, A is drained. It stays up for its longest declared wait, for late messages. Only then may it be removed. Removal is refused while a pinned payment is open. The package stays on GitHub and in the store, as a record.

22
00:05:47,735 --> 00:06:03,799
Rollback is just another activation: activate the earlier distribution again. If it was removed, reinstall it from GitHub first. Payments that started on the newer one finish on it. Every one of these changes is a new row, with who did it and when.

23
00:06:05,415 --> 00:06:19,559
Inside a distribution, what does a bank actually run? Platform workflows. The corporate payment workflow validates, screens, checks limits, reserves the funds and routes the payment. It has a slot, the scheme leg, which a scheme fills.

24
00:06:21,171 --> 00:06:34,931
A scheme's dispatch table says which message starts which workflow, and which leg fills the slot. For WPS, a salary file starts an outward corporate payment, and the WPS leg fills its scheme leg.

25
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:56,209
The same leg idea works for refunds. A refund is an order of its own, on the platform's refund workflow. The platform finds the earlier payment, prices, approves and posts. WPS is the leg that answers and returns the funds. The refund cycle is only partly built, and its layouts are not confirmed.

26
00:06:57,814 --> 00:07:11,467
A bank adds its own logic at hook points, for example before release, without touching the platform workflow. Its rules use parameters: every value a bank may change has a default and bounds, and is never a constant.

27
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:26,008
One rule ties it together. Values are looked up by date, so a new value can reach a payment already in flight, where its rule says so. Logic is pinned, so a running payment never changes the code it runs.

28
00:07:27,612 --> 00:07:37,318
Files. Every file the runtime receives or sends goes through one service, the File API. Nothing else reads a partner's folder or writes to one.

29
00:07:38,931 --> 00:07:58,344
Files come in four ways: a channel's upload over HTTPS, a person's upload on a screen, the SFTP gateway, and the inboxes it polls. On the laptop the inbox is a folder. At a bank it is the partner's SFTP folder, reached by the File API's own SFTP adapter.

30
00:07:59,949 --> 00:08:19,362
Behind the intake is the front door. A file that starts a payment pins it to the bank's active distribution, reads it with the scheme's intake map, and starts it on the engine host. A file for a running payment, such as an acknowledgement, is matched to its payment, and handed to the host of the distribution it is pinned to.

31
00:08:20,973 --> 00:08:39,212
Going out is the same door in reverse. A workflow's file goes to the File API's outbox, is kept with its payment, and is delivered by the bank's binding: SFTP to the Central Bank or the ministry, or a folder on a laptop. A resend from a console sends the kept bytes again.

32
00:08:40,827 --> 00:08:53,733
Some file rules, such as allowed names and sizes, are still settings of the installation, because the scheme does not declare them yet. And the bank's partners' SFTP host keys are waiting on each bank.

33
00:08:55,335 --> 00:09:08,455
Now, where things are authored, and where they run. The studio is multi-tenant. The platform vendor, the scheme authors and every bank work in it, each in their own tenant, and each bank sees only its own content.

34
00:09:10,068 --> 00:09:23,358
The studio keeps no database of its own. Its only store is one GitHub branch, folder content configurations. A change in progress is a branch of its own, and a published file is never rewritten.

35
00:09:24,959 --> 00:09:40,766
The studio builds a distribution for every bank, and signs it. Each bank has its own runtime. A runtime is told its bank when it is installed, and refuses another bank's distribution, configuration release, file or backup, whoever asks.

36
00:09:42,381 --> 00:09:55,223
Last, who may change what. In the studio, every change is maker and checker. The person who made a change never approves it. A bank configurator makes the bank's change, and a bank approver approves it.

37
00:09:56,826 --> 00:10:16,644
In the runtime, every change of the system is asked for by one person and carried out only when another person, with the bank approver's role, consents. Installing, activating, draining, removing, rolling back, restoring, unmasking a field, changing a register. Each request and each consent is a fact.

38
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:27,348
An operator's decision on a payment, such as approving a hold, is a business decision instead. It keeps the second approver its view declares.

39
00:10:28,967 --> 00:10:51,900
What is not built yet, and matters. The owner has just approved four more changes, which are being built now: bank values reaching a runtime only through signed configuration releases with a bank approver's consent; native code carried in each distribution, not in the runtime image; the engine host reading the bank's values; and ports for notifications and event streaming.

40
00:10:53,514 --> 00:11:13,610
Also open: durable DTFx and Elsa runs, the scheme's own file rules, and the counterparts each bank must still name, such as its core banking settings and screening engines. Other schemes, such as IPI, are deferred by the owner. WPS itself is provisional until its rulebook confirms it.

41
00:11:15,228 --> 00:11:33,489
That is the north-star architecture: one model, one insert-only store, four engines behind one contract, three layers, pinned payments, one door for files, and two people for every change. In the next video, we follow one payment through all of it, from the studio to the operations console.
